<section aria-labelledby="assessment-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <h2
      class="section-title"
      id="assessment-heading">
      Professional Growth and Career Preparation
    </h2>

    <p class="section-intro">
      This self-assessment summarizes how my academic work, capstone
      enhancements, and professional experiences prepared me to enter
      and contribute to the computer science field.
    </p>

    <article class="about-card">
      <p>
        When I began my academic career at Southern New Hampshire
        University, I was all ambition with no knowledge. Now, after
        completing the program and developing my ePortfolio, I have
        transformed the way I view myself as a technology professional.
        I entered the program with no understanding of software
        development, databases, or secure coding. Through my coursework,
        independent practice, and capstone enhancements, I developed the
        ability to design, evaluate, improve, test, and communicate
        software solutions. My primary career goal is to apply these
        skills in healthcare technology, particularly in implementation
        analysis, healthcare software support, electronic medical record
        systems, and application development.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        The coursework prepared me for the challenges I would encounter
        when entering and working in the technology field. I built
        technical skills in programming, software development, testing,
        databases, cybersecurity, and system design. The projects helped
        move me toward my goal of working in healthcare IT while also
        expanding my interests into areas such as cybersecurity and
        governance, risk, and compliance. I experienced the value of
        these skills firsthand when I was hired as an implementation
        analyst for a medical software company. During the hiring
        process, I used the projects and knowledge I gained throughout
        the program to speak confidently and professionally about my
        work and the skills I could offer.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        Although much of my coursework was completed independently, the
        program taught me practices that are directly applicable to team
        environments. These include version control, readable code,
        technical documentation, code reviews, testing records, clear
        naming conventions, and organized repositories. My professional
        experience outside the technology industry also helped me build
        strong collaborative and communication skills. Many courses
        prepared me to interact with nontechnical audiences by requiring
        me to break down technical information for instructors,
        stakeholders, and potential employers. These experiences taught
        me that effective software development depends not only on
        technical ability, but also on the ability to listen, clarify
        requirements, document decisions, and explain solutions in a way
        that different audiences can understand.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        Working on enhancements to my previous projects during the
        capstone helped me better understand how to choose data
        structures based on the problem being solved. Throughout the
        program, I worked with arrays, lists, objects, trees, maps,
        search methods, sorting, iteration, and performance analysis.
        Consistent algorithm practice improved my ability to break
        problems into inputs, conditions, iterations, outputs, and edge
        cases. I also learned to evaluate time complexity and
        scalability when deciding which approach to use. The Travlr
        enhancement demonstrated this growth through keyword searching,
        numeric filtering, and sorting by multiple fields. This work
        showed me that algorithms are not limited to abstract coding
        exercises; they are present in practical application features
        that help users locate, organize, and understand data.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        I began the Computer Science program having completed only a
        beginner's Python course. I now have experience with Java,
        Android Studio, SQLite, Angular, TypeScript, Node.js, Express,
        MongoDB, Mongoose, REST APIs, and Postman. I learned how to
        separate user-interface logic, business logic, validation, and
        database operations. My database coursework taught me about
        schemas, data types, constraints, indexes, query performance,
        and data integrity. The capstone enhancements reinforced that
        database design is not merely about storing information. It also
        involves ensuring that records are valid, unique when required,
        efficiently retrievable, and protected from unauthorized
        changes.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        The projects completed in my Secure Coding course taught me how
        to anticipate misuse, invalid input, unauthorized access, and
        exposure of sensitive information. The knowledge I gained in
        that course influenced the security enhancements I made to my
        capstone artifacts. In the Android Weight Management
        application, I strengthened input validation, replaced plaintext
        password storage with password hashing, and considered
        permissions when handling phone-number data. In the Travlr
        full-stack application, I added database-backed authentication,
        hashed and salted passwords, JWT authentication, role-based
        authorization, protected CRUD routes, and safer error responses.
        In healthcare, robust security practices are essential because
        organizations work with patients' sensitive health information.
        I also strengthened my knowledge outside university coursework
        by studying for and passing the CompTIA Security+ examination.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        I selected the capstone artifacts because they demonstrate
        skills in both mobile and full-stack web development. The Weight
        Management Android Application represents software engineering
        and design. The Travlr Getaways application represents
        algorithms and data structures as well as databases. Together,
        the artifacts tell a progression story: identifying weaknesses,
        planning improvements, implementing enhancements,
        troubleshooting problems, testing results, and explaining the
        value of the completed work. The accompanying code review and
        narratives further demonstrate my ability to evaluate existing
        software, communicate technical decisions, and reflect on my
        growth.
      </p>

      <p class="assessment-paragraph">
        Overall, the Computer Science program and capstone strengthened
        both my technical abilities and my professional confidence. I am
        leaving the program with evidence that I can analyze complex
        systems, learn unfamiliar tools, communicate with technical and
        nontechnical audiences, protect data, and persist through
        difficult implementation problems. These strengths position me
        to contribute to healthcare technology teams while continuing
        to grow in software development, database systems, security, and
        emerging technologies.
      </p>

